WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.223
One photo of a football boot.

00:00:02.223 --> 00:00:04.588
In the middle, what TRELLIS.2 makes of it.

00:00:04.588 --> 00:00:07.543
On the right, the same model with two small adapters.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:11.324
Hi, I'm Insole!

00:00:11.324 --> 00:00:14.479
Hugging Face published a guide to fine-tuning a 3D generator.

00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:18.265
It is about one model and one kind of object, and that

00:00:18.265 --> 00:00:19.843
is what makes it useful.

00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:25.404
TRELLIS.2 turns a photo into a mesh in three stages, each a

00:00:25.404 --> 00:00:27.749
flow transformer reading the same image tokens.

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:31.583
Stage one decides which voxels are part of the object at all.

00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:32.436
Its coarse shape.

00:00:32.856 --> 00:00:36.085
Stage two makes the surface geometry for every occupied cell.

00:00:36.085 --> 00:00:37.377
The sole lives here.

00:00:37.797 --> 00:00:39.384
Stage three paints the materials.

00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:40.971
The guide leaves it frozen.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.151
The scans only have colour with the lighting baked in.

00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:50.046
And the error is in the soles, not the colours.

00:00:50.046 --> 00:00:52.128
So only the geometry stages get adapters.

00:00:53.525 --> 00:00:56.303
LoRA adds a low rank update to each targeted layer.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.427
The original weight stays frozen.

00:00:59.575 --> 00:01:03.210
The adapter learns two thin matrices whose product is the update.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.150
B starts at zero, so training begins exactly at the base model.

00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:10.929
I read the two released adapters' headers.

00:01:10.929 --> 00:01:13.433
Each holds nine point three million parameters.

00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:15.938
The guide says that number covers both.

00:01:15.938 --> 00:01:17.011
It covers one.

00:01:18.558 --> 00:01:22.815
The data is a hundred and eighty-eight scanned shoes from Google Scanned Objects.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:27.365
TRELLIS.2's own frozen encoders turn each mesh into latents once, so

00:01:27.365 --> 00:01:28.874
every run reuses them.

00:01:29.274 --> 00:01:32.939
Blender renders sixteen views of each shoe, and training picks one at

00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:34.466
random as the input photo.

00:01:34.866 --> 00:01:38.742
Nine shoes are held out, with their other colourways kept out of training too.

00:01:39.142 --> 00:01:41.683
Then one run per adapter on an A100.

00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:45.496
The guide's runs took seven point two and three point six hours.

00:01:47.492 --> 00:01:48.430
Now the scores.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.870
The evaluation centres and scales each mesh, but never rotates it.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:56.247
A shoe lying on its side scores badly even if its shape is right.

00:01:56.667 --> 00:02:00.361
Turn the base output upright, and on five held-out shoes its F-score

00:02:00.361 --> 00:02:01.592
climbs to nearly half.

00:02:01.592 --> 00:02:02.823
That part was pose.

00:02:03.243 --> 00:02:06.258
The LoRA reaches about nought point eight five on the same five.

00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:07.514
That gap is the sole.

00:02:09.258 --> 00:02:12.804
The guide's own figure shows both: the base as generated, then

00:02:12.804 --> 00:02:14.094
at its best rotation.

00:02:14.514 --> 00:02:16.417
And with the adapters, next to the scan.

00:02:18.358 --> 00:02:22.067
The headline says F-score went from about a quarter to about four fifths.

00:02:22.487 --> 00:02:25.392
Part of that is the adapters learning to stand shoes the

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:26.449
way the scans stand.

00:02:26.869 --> 00:02:30.151
The rest is better soles, which is the part the guide was after.

00:02:31.558 --> 00:02:34.858
Pick the stage your error lives in, freeze the rest, and align

00:02:34.858 --> 00:02:36.508
your meshes before you score them.

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:43.259
Two geometry adapters, a frozen texture stage, and pose and shape scored apart.

00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:45.823
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:45.823 --> 00:02:46.436
I'm Insole.

00:02:46.436 --> 00:02:46.743
Bye!
